{"name":"Smoky Quartz","hex":"#51484f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smoky quartz","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brown to black variety of quartz."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoky_quartz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Smoky quartz","blocks":[{"text":"Smoky quartz is a brown to black, translucent variety of quartz. It ranges in clarity from almost complete transparency to almost-opaque black crystals. Smoky quartz is popular as a gemstone and as a collectible crystal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Smoky-quartz-TUCQTZ09-03-arkenstone-irocks.png/330px-Smoky-quartz-TUCQTZ09-03-arkenstone-irocks.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoky_quartz","wikidata":"Q429935"},{"title":"Quartz","blocks":[{"text":"Quartz is a hard mineral composed of silica (silicon dioxide). Its atoms are linked in a continuous framework of SiO4 silicon–oxygen tetrahedra, with each oxygen atom being shared between two tetrahedra, giving an overall chemical formula of SiO2. Therefore, quartz is classified structurally as a framework silicate mineral and compositionally as an oxide mineral. Quartz is the second most common mineral or mineral group in Earth's lithosphere, comprising about 12% by mass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Quartz_Br%C3%A9sil.jpg/330px-Quartz_Br%C3%A9sil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartz","wikidata":"Q43010"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Alpha-quartz%2C_P3_121_and_P3_221.png/500px-Alpha-quartz%2C_P3_121_and_P3_221.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Alpha-quartz%2C_P3_121_and_P3_221.png/1280px-Alpha-quartz%2C_P3_121_and_P3_221.png?utm_source